WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.803 --> 00:00:14.899
Lady Macbeth, along with all the guests, stared at her husband.

2
00:00:14.901 --> 00:00:21.829
A murmur was spreading through the hall. "Did he say ‘Banquo's ghost'?" whispered some.

3
00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:24.416
"He said Banquo should be dead!"

4
00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:28.083
"Who do you see, my lord?" Ross asked the king.

5
00:00:28.084 --> 00:00:35.107
But Lady Macbeth quickly interrupted. "Please, such questions will only worsen his condition."

6
00:00:35.108 --> 00:00:43.678
Then, turning to all the guests she said, "I'm sorry that our evening has been spoiled," and she wished them all a kind goodnight.

7
00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:48.147
Attendants ushered the guests out of the hall, leaving Macbeth and his wife alone.

8
00:00:48.148 --> 00:00:54.378
"The ghost is gone; I am myself again," he reassured her. "Is it late?"

9
00:00:54.379 --> 00:00:57.409
Quietly, they began to talk about the evening.

10
00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:04.832
Lady Macbeth pointed out that Macduff had been absent. "He refused our invitation," she said.

11
00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:08.325
"It makes me suspect him," agreed Macbeth.

12
00:01:08.326 --> 00:01:14.372
Macbeth kept spies in the houses of all the Scottish noblemen, but he trusted Macduff the least.

13
00:01:14.373 --> 00:01:18.666
The more they talked, the more fears and suspicions grew in his mind.

14
00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:24.277
Before he and Lady Macbeth went to bed, he resolved to visit the three witches once again.

15
00:01:24.278 --> 00:01:29.550
"I must know the worst," he said, "even by the worst means."

16
00:01:29.551 --> 00:01:33.991
The witches, of course, knew that Macbeth was coming long before he arrived.

17
00:01:33.992 --> 00:01:40.158
In their dark cave, a huge cauldron bubbled over a fire. They circled around it, chanting:

18
00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:45.714
Round about the cauldron go; into it, poisoned entrails throw.

19
00:01:45.715 --> 00:01:50.683
Eye of newt and toe of frog, wool of bat and tongue of dog,

20
00:01:50.685 --> 00:01:55.605
Baboon's blood and tail of snake, in the cauldron boil and bake.

21
00:01:55.606 --> 00:02:00.623
Double, double, toil and trouble; fire burn and cauldron bubble!

22
00:02:00.623 --> 00:02:05.145
Then, using a thick bone, they stirred the stinking pot together.

23
00:02:05.146 --> 00:02:12.462
Near the heath where he first saw the witches, a day's journey from the palace, Macbeth found the entrance to their cave.

24
00:02:12.463 --> 00:02:15.709
A foul smell wafted from the dark opening.

25
00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:21.129
He covered his nose with a corner of his cloak and, ducking his head, stepped inside.

26
00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:27.566
An oily smoke burned his eyes, but he could see the three ugly witches in the red light of the cauldron's fire.

27
00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:32.097
"What is it you do, you black and unholy hags?" he asked.

28
00:02:32.098 --> 00:02:37.383
"A deed without a name," said the first witch with a crooked smile.

29
00:02:37.384 --> 00:02:40.251
"Come!" commanded the second witch.

30
00:02:40.252 --> 00:02:43.519
"We'll answer you," said the third.

31
00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:50.129
Macbeth stepped closer and watched as the last witch dropped something red and wriggling into the bubbling liquid.

32
00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:55.653
A dark smoke rose, and Macbeth saw a ghostly head materialize in the air.

33
00:02:55.654 --> 00:02:59.168
It wore a helmet, as if armed for war.

34
00:02:59.169 --> 00:03:04.739
"Tell me, you unknown power―" Macbeth began, but the first witch interrupted him.

35
00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:08.787
"He knows your thoughts. Listen and speak not."

36
00:03:08.788 --> 00:03:15.390
"Macbeth! Macbeth! Beware Macduff!" said the vision.

37
00:03:15.391 --> 00:03:19.764
"It knows my fear and cautions me well," said Macbeth.

38
00:03:19.764 --> 00:03:25.065
"Just one word more—" but before he could ask, the vision descended into the pot.

39
00:03:25.066 --> 00:03:30.946
A second apparition rose from the cauldron. This one was in the form of a bloody child.

40
00:03:30.947 --> 00:03:40.788
"Macbeth! Macbeth! Laugh and scorn the power of men," it said, "for none born of woman shall harm Macbeth."

41
00:03:40.789 --> 00:03:43.946
Then it too descended into the creeping vapors.

42
00:03:43.947 --> 00:03:50.208
A third vision rose. This time it was a child wearing a crown and carrying a branch in its hand.

43
00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:54.629
It spoke: "Take no care, whoever your enemies are.

44
00:03:54.630 --> 00:04:01.232
Macbeth shall never be defeated until great Birnam Wood comes to high Dunsinane Hill."

45
00:04:01.233 --> 00:04:09.796
"That will never be!" Macbeth whispered excitedly. "How can the trees pull up their roots and march against my castle there?

46
00:04:09.797 --> 00:04:19.344
And ‘none born of woman' shall harm me? Then, Macduff, I have no reason to fear you . . . But just in case, you will not live."

47
00:04:19.345 --> 00:04:22.780
He turned to look back at the cauldron, but the witches were gone.

48
00:04:22.781 --> 00:04:26.320
The fire was out and the cave was suddenly cold and desolate.

49
00:04:26.321 --> 00:04:33.693
Outside, the air was damp and fresh. Feeling as if he had awakened from a drugged sleep, Macbeth inhaled deeply.

50
00:04:33.694 --> 00:04:37.177
On the long journey back to the palace, his thoughts churned.

51
00:04:37.178 --> 00:04:41.518
"They are the devil's sisters, I'm sure, but they make me bold!

52
00:04:41.519 --> 00:04:46.152
From now on, the first thought of my heart will be the first act of my hand!

53
00:04:46.153 --> 00:04:52.362
I have waded so deep into a river of blood that to go back is just as far as to go on."

54
00:04:52.363 --> 00:04:59.342
His face grew grim. "‘Beware Macduff,' they warned me. I must act on this advice!"

55
00:04:59.343 --> 00:05:03.017
When he arrived at the palace, a messenger was waiting for him.

56
00:05:03.018 --> 00:05:08.319
It was his spy from Macduff's castle with news that Macduff had fled to England.

57
00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:13.279
Macbeth clenched his fists. "He has anticipated my plan!"

58
00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:20.747
In England, Duncan's oldest son, Malcolm, had taken refuge at the court of the king, where many were sympathetic to his cause.

59
00:05:20.748 --> 00:05:26.422
Since the day Macbeth had come to power, the news from Scotland had grown steadily worse.

60
00:05:26.423 --> 00:05:32.144
The Scottish nobles feared him. And anyone who Macbeth suspected lived in terror of his army.

61
00:05:32.145 --> 00:05:37.112
Rumors of Macbeth's murders were spreading. People whispered to each other about war.

62
00:05:37.113 --> 00:05:42.160
With the help of the English lords, Malcolm was slowly gathering an army of his own.

63
00:05:42.161 --> 00:05:47.326
The king of England had offered him ten thousand men, and more were joining every day.

64
00:05:47.327 --> 00:05:53.787
When Macduff arrived at the English court, he immediately offered to help the young prince take back his father's throne.

65
00:05:53.788 --> 00:06:00.175
"Macbeth is a tyrant," said Macduff, as he strolled with Malcolm outside the English court.

66
00:06:00.176 --> 00:06:07.524
"Every morning, new widows mourn and new orphans cry. Scotland suffers badly under his rule."

67
00:06:07.525 --> 00:06:15.119
"Others hear our country's cry," said Malcolm. "The English lords Siward and Northumberland have come to our side."

68
00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:21.334
Just then, a man approached from across the lawn, and they quickly recognized Ross, their fellow countryman.

69
00:06:21.335 --> 00:06:26.375
After they greeted each other, Macduff asked, "How do things stand in Scotland?"

70
00:06:26.376 --> 00:06:33.017
Ross shook his head. "Our homeland is becoming a graveyard. There are new atrocities every day."

71
00:06:33.018 --> 00:06:38.858
"How are my wife and children?" asked Macduff. "Macbeth has not assaulted their peace?"

72
00:06:38.859 --> 00:06:44.874
Ross paused. "I have words that I would rather howl in the desert than tell you.

73
00:06:44.875 --> 00:06:50.493
Fife Castle has been attacked, and your wife and children savagely slaughtered."

74
00:06:50.494 --> 00:06:56.960
"Merciful heaven!" exclaimed Malcolm, but Macduff was stunned and silent.

75
00:06:56.961 --> 00:07:03.555
There was a pause. "What, man! Give words to your grief," said Malcolm.

76
00:07:03.556 --> 00:07:06.576
"My children?" asked Macduff.

77
00:07:06.576 --> 00:07:13.278
"Wife, children, servants—all that Macbeth's murderers found," said Ross.

78
00:07:13.279 --> 00:07:18.783
"Oh, torment and hell! My wife killed too?" cried Macduff.

79
00:07:18.784 --> 00:07:20.956
Ross nodded solemnly.

80
00:07:20.957 --> 00:07:24.902
"My children! Not all? Please, not all!"

81
00:07:24.902 --> 00:07:28.226
"You must bear it like a man," said Malcolm.

82
00:07:28.227 --> 00:07:34.346
Macduff turned to him angrily. "I shall do so! But I must feel it as a man, too.

83
00:07:34.347 --> 00:07:39.924
There was never anything so precious to me as my sweet family. Gone!"

84
00:07:39.925 --> 00:07:49.210
He turned from them and strode away. Then he turned back. "This grief is a whetstone to sharpen my sword!" he said.

85
00:07:49.211 --> 00:07:57.042
"I won't let this sadness blunt my heart. Oh, I could fill my eyes with tears and cry sorrows with my tongue.

86
00:07:57.043 --> 00:08:06.351
But enough! Bring this demon of Scotland face to face with me, set him within reach of my sword, and heaven forgive me if he escapes death!"
